Paste in the editor on WebKit.

The editor's paste falls back to navigator.clipboard.readText() on web, which
WebKit refuses there ("Unable to read from the browser's clipboard") even though
the very same gesture copies fine via execCommand. Fill in the triggerPaste()
hook - upstream's own opt-out from that fallback, a stub in the browser and
nativeHost in Electron - with execCommand('paste'), which WebKit alone
implements behind its native paste callout. That yields a real paste event
carrying clipboardData, the mechanism copy already uses here.

It returns undefined when refused, so every other engine keeps the readText path
unchanged. A vendor defect, kept individually deletable: delete this patch the
day WebKit allows the clipboard read the rest of the page already gets.

--- a/lib/vscode/src/vs/platform/clipboard/browser/clipboardService.ts
+++ b/lib/vscode/src/vs/platform/clipboard/browser/clipboardService.ts
@@ -47,7 +47,21 @@ export class BrowserClipboardService extends Disposable implements IClipboardSer
 
 	triggerPaste(): Promise<void> | undefined {
 		this.logService.trace('BrowserClipboardService#triggerPaste');
-		return undefined;
+		// Composery: WebKit is the only engine implementing programmatic paste
+		// (execCommand('paste'), behind its native paste callout, since Safari 13). It
+		// produces a real paste event carrying clipboardData - the same mechanism the
+		// editor's copy already uses successfully on iOS - whereas the readText() the
+		// editor otherwise falls back to is a separate permission model that WebKit
+		// refuses here, surfacing as "Unable to read from the browser's clipboard".
+		// Refusal returns undefined, leaving that fallback exactly as it was for every
+		// other engine (Chromium grants the read, so Android is untouched), so this can
+		// only add a way to paste, never remove one.
+		if (isSafari || isWebkitWebView) {
+			if (getActiveDocument().execCommand('paste')) {
+				return Promise.resolve();
+			}
+		}
+		return undefined;
 	}
 
 	async readImage(): Promise<Uint8Array> {
